(>> 4.5 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Contrast 'var', 'let', and 'const' with respect to scope, variable hoisting,) Tj
T*
(and the Temporal Dead Zone \(TDZ\).) Tj
T*
(Answer: 'var' is function-scoped; when declared inside an if-block or loop, it leaks) Tj
T*
(into the enclosing function. 'var' declarations are hoisted to the top of their scope) Tj
T*
(and initialized to 'undefined', allowing access before declaration without error. 'let') Tj
T*
(and 'const' \(introduced in ES6\) are block-scoped, existing strictly within their) Tj
T*
(enclosing curly braces {}. While 'let' and 'const' are also hoisted by the JS engine) Tj
T*
(during creation phase, they are NOT initialized. The time between entering the block) Tj
T*
(scope and encountering the actual declaration statement is the Temporal Dead Zone \(TDZ\).) Tj
T*
(Accessing a 'let' or 'const' variable in the TDZ throws a fatal ReferenceError. 'const') Tj
T*
(further mandates immediate initialization and forbids re-assignment.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the concept of Closures in JavaScript and demonstrate how closures can) Tj
T*
(be used to emulate private object properties.) Tj
T*
(Answer: A closure is the persistent lexical scope bundle formed when an inner function) Tj
T*
(retains access to variables declared in its outer enclosing function, even after that) Tj
T*
(outer function has returned and exited the call stack. Because JavaScript historically) Tj
T*
(lacked native 'private' class keywords, closures provide the canonical mechanism for) Tj
T*
(data encapsulation. In a factory function, local variables defined inside the outer) Tj
T*
(function cannot be accessed or modified directly by external code; they can only be read) Tj
T*
(or manipulated via inner methods exposed in the returned public object, creating true) Tj
T*
